  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE...Warm front lifts north through the afternoon.\n\nLatest analysis shows a small meso-low feature near Manassas \nVA, and this feature will likely drift northeastward just \nnorthwest of US-29 toward Baltimore's northwest suburbs through \nthe afternoon.\n\nOn radar and satellite, this feature has the appearance of an\noccluded low. The pseudo \"triple point\" near Baltimore is where\nthe strongest convergence remains.\n\nThe highest PW axis is overhead, so anything that develops will\nbe capable of very heavy rainfall rates. Slow storm \nmotions/training are possible as this weak low moves overhead of\nnortheast MD. Forcing for ascent via moisture flux has ceased, \nso further development will rely on subtle convergence axes \nand/or heating of an extremely humid airmass through limited \ncloud breaks.\n\nHave cancelled the watch as convergence continues to decrease\nsouthwest of Baltimore. Meanwhile, convergence and CAPE will \nremain in the Baltimore area through this afternoon. The last \nfew runs of the HRRR maintain some redevelopment through mid\nafternoon, though the strongest convergence is showing signs of\nlifting out as of just before 2PM EDT.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Daily shower and thunderstorm chances are expected \nthrough early next week.\n\nIn the wake of what morning convection materializes, the models do \nshow a brief lull in the action. The northward pushing warm front \neventually stalls nearby before shearing out. Likewise, the upper \nlow across the Mid-South region begins to fill although the \nreflection of this circulation still shows up across southern West \nVirginia. \n\nA deep southerly flow will continue around high pressure over the \nAtlantic, ushering plenty of moisture. PWATS will be near or greater \nthan 2\" in the valleys with 1-2\" in the mountains, which is about 1-\n2 SD above climo. A surface trough and shortwave energy will trigger \nscattered showers/thunderstorms, but it should not be a washout \nduring the afternoon/evening. The main threat will be brief heavy \ndownpours and gusty winds. However, DCAPE and shear are on the lower \nside, so the probability of severe storms is lower. If some storms \ntrain over the same area, localized flash flooding can occur. The \npattern does favor the possibility for training convection (cloud \nlayer winds nearly parallel to low-level wind), but the coverage \nshould be isolated. \n\nA similar pattern will hold on for Thursday and Friday while the \nsubtropical jet strengthens to the north and west. Therefore, more \nshowers and thunderstorms are likely along with the threat for \nisolated instances of flash flooding and locally damaging wind \ngusts. Hotter conditions may cause relatively steeper low-level \nlapse rates and the wind field strengthens a bit (especially \nFriday). \n\nA quasi-zonal flow aloft will develop for the weekend into next \nweek, bringing more seasonably hot and humid conditions along with \ndaily chances for showers and thunderstorms.",
